{"id":"subsets-ii","title":"Subsets II","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Backtracking","tags":["Array","Backtracking","Sorting"],"companies":{"google":2,"meta":2,"amazon":2,"microsoft":1},"description":"Given an integer array `nums` that **may contain duplicates**, return every distinct subset (the power set without repeats).\n\nTwo subsets are the same if they contain the same values with the same multiplicities, so `[1,2]` and `[2,1]` count as one. Return the subsets in any order.","examples":[{"input":"nums = [1,2,2]","output":"[[],[1],[1,2],[1,2,2],[2],[2,2]]","explanation":"Picking the first 2 alone or the second 2 alone gives the same subset [2], so it appears once."},{"input":"nums = [0]","output":"[[],[0]]"}],"constraints":["0 <= nums.length <= 14","-10 <= nums[i] <= 10"],"hints":["Start from the Subsets solution and think about exactly which branch creates a duplicate.","Sort first. At a given recursion depth, choosing nums[i] when nums[i] == nums[i-1] and i > start repeats a subset you already built from nums[i-1].","So inside the loop: `if i > start and nums[i] == nums[i-1]: continue`. Equal values are still allowed at *different* depths, which is how [2,2] is produced."],"function":"subsetsWithDup","signature":"def subsetsWithDup(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:","arg_types":{"nums":"List[int]"},"return_type":"List[List[int]]","compare":"unordered_nested","tests":[{"input":{"nums":[1,2,2]},"output":[[],[1],[1,2],[1,2,2],[2],[2,2]]},{"input":{"nums":[0]},"output":[[],[0]]},{"input":{"nums":[]},"output":[[]]},{"input":{"nums":[2,2,2]},"output":[[],[2],[2,2],[2,2,2]]},{"input":{"nums":[4,4,1,4]},"output":[[],[1],[1,4],[1,4,4],[1,4,4,4],[4],[4,4],[4,4,4]]},{"input":{"nums":[1,2,1,3]},"output":[[],[1],[1,1],[1,1,2],[1,1,2,3],[1,1,3],[1,2],[1,2,3],[1,3],[2],[2,3],[3]]},{"input":{"nums":[7,1,6,2,5,3,4,4,3,5,2,6,1,7]},"output":[[],[7],[7,7],[6],[6,7],[6,7,7],[6,6],[6,6,7],[6,6,7,7],[5],[5,7],[5,7,7],[5,6],[5,6,7],[5,6,7,7],[5,6,6],[5,6,6,7],[5,6,6,7,7]